WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.468
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:03.769
you know from his work on
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:05.738
He's also the host of
"America's Got Talent,"

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:08.208
which airs Tuesday nights
at 8:00 here on NBC.

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.243
Here's my conversation
with Terry Crews.

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:13.012
Terry, it's always so lovely
to see you.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.315
I know from every one
of our interactions,

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:18.017
you are the most positive,
optimistic guy.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:21.253
You can provide a unique
perspective on a lot of things.

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:25.191
But one thing is, you played in
the NFL from '91 to '97.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.694
And I'm wondering
what your reaction was

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:31.597
to Commissioner Roger Goodell
saying last week, basically,

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:33.098
"We got it wrong.

00:00:33.098 --> 00:00:35.968
We should do a better job
of listening to our players."

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:38.905
-You know,
it was the same feeling I felt

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:41.540
when I saw Obama's inauguration.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.943
I thought
I would never see this.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:47.713
This is a --
It was a moment that, you know,

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:50.116
it stands in time,
even to this day --

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:53.085
to watch, you know,
Roger Goodell actually admit

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:55.354
that they were wrong

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:59.525
because, again,
I left the NFL 23 years ago,

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:03.563
and it was -- it was --
it still is, but, you know,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:07.000
racism was prevalent
all the way throughout.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.936
And, you know, but there was
a lot of indignities

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.971
that I had to just put up with

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.840
simply because I wanted to,
you know,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.774
make conditions better
for my family.

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:16.943
That's the way it was.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:20.546
And to see this happen,
and especially --

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:22.047
and one of the big things,

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:25.718
and I have to call, you know,
Roger on this, though,

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:29.489
is the fact that they did not
mention Colin Kaepernick.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:30.657
-Right.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:35.327
-Which is still the most glaring
example of racism

00:01:35.327 --> 00:01:37.396
that the NFL has ever had,

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:39.799
and they could correct it.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:42.868
But I'm really, really hoping

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:46.973
that Kaepernick gets a chance
to show himself on a NFL field,

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:48.841
which would really mean healing.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:52.879
It would really take, you know,
the NFL seriously

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.914
on everything
they're saying right now.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.016
-You know, I think a lot of us
watch the NFL,

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.118
and we see white players,
and we see black players,

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:02.254
and we think, "Oh, this
is a great example of diversity,

00:02:02.254 --> 00:02:03.489
because, obviously,
the league

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.357
is a meritocracy,
you would think,

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:06.992
and the best players play.

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:09.529
But can you speak a little
to how black players

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:10.797
were treated differently
than white players

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:11.931
in your years in the league?

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:14.533
-When we were in the locker room

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.101
and you were in
the training room,

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:20.873
basically, you had an injury,
it was always viewed,

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.809
in regards to the black players,
that we were faking,

00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.578
that we were trying to
get out of work,

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:30.350
that we were trying to skip the
game and, yet, get paid anyway.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:34.820
And with the white players,
we noticed this whole thing of

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.455
if they were injured,
it was because,

00:02:36.455 --> 00:02:39.692
oh, man, you know,
it was a real injury.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:43.463
It was something that we're
going to get you back from.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.265
But this was a through line
in the league.

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:48.935
You know, another thing that
happened to me in the NFL is --

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:53.406
I had a coach when I was --
My second year in the league,

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:56.075
he decided he wanted
to call me Tyrone.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.343
And this is a white guy.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.913
And I said, "No, sir.
My name is Terry."

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:03.282
He was like, "No, I like Tyrone.
I'm going to call you Tyrone."

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:07.987
And you're talking about this
whole mood of, you know,

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.423
even when we were out there,
out on the field,

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:11.924
you know,
they're walking the line.

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:13.793
It felt like a plantation.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.828
And you felt like you
could be bought and sold,

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.363
and you actually were.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.164
If a lot of NFL players realized

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:21.034
they could do more, they would.

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:23.870
You know, it's like, "We could
go on and do something else,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.040
because who wants to get knocked
out on Monday Night Football?"

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.241
You know what I mean?

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:32.578
But my thing was, is that once
I discovered that

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:36.215
I did have more,
it lost its power, you know?

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:39.652
It was one of those things I was
like, "I can go and I'm fine."

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:44.156
And I think more players
right now realize the power

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:48.127
that they have more than any
other time in NFL history.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.663
Especially, you know,
with the current events

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:52.999
that happened,
you know, just lately,

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.201
it's a wake-up call
for the league,

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:58.171
because, again, you are talking
more than 60% of the players

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:01.674
are black, but there are
no black owners,

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.509
very few black coaches,

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.712
and it still, still
needs to change,

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:08.614
I mean, immediately.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:13.519
-You were on the Rams in 1992,
during the riots then.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:16.389
Pulled over by the police then.
-That's right.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.158
-And being a player for
the L.A. Rams

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.360
did not provide you
any safety in that.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.761
-Not at all.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:26.432
In fact, you know, we were in
Orange County at the time,

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.501
and I was driving into L.A.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.403
It was after the riots.

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:33.138
And I was pulled over by
a couple cop cars,

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:34.574
which really shocked me.

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:36.475
It was like, "There's two here."

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:41.114
And I was told to put
all four of my windows down,

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.648
keep my hands at 10:00 and 2:00,

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:45.518
and those officers
came out of their car

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:50.022
with their guns
pointed right at my head.

00:04:50.022 --> 00:04:53.626
I can't even describe
to you how it felt.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.461
I mean, you are so flustered.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.496
You don't know if
you are doing things right

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.031
or you are doing things wrong.

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:02.167
They are barking things through
this megaphone about, you know,

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.870
"Put your right hand
into your pocket.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.874
Get out your identification,
put it out the window."

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:10.476
You know, just like,
if I make one wrong move,

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:14.514
the whole thing is,
you realize I could die.

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:17.416
If they don't understand
my moves,

00:05:17.416 --> 00:05:21.220
if they misconstrue
any move that I am making,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.723
they would feel
justified to shoot.

